  "Q1": {
    "answer": "Peskas is connected to a variety of entities and initiatives. It references WorldFish, Fish & Aquatic-Food Nutrition, Fish Aggregating Devices (FADs), the Asia-Africa BlueTech Superhighway (AABS), IUU Fishing Monitoring, Harvard Dataverse, Nairobi Convention, and the countries Mozambique, Timor-Leste, Zanzibar, Kenya, Tanzania, and Malawi. Peskas is also conceptually related to Functional Requirements for Electronic Data Collection Tools and PeskAAS (Peskas Automated Analytics System). The Peskas COASTS (regional analysis platform) is part of Peskas, though this connection is inferred. Peskas is produced by Alexander Tilley and produces the Zanzibar Validated Trips, Mozambique Validated Trips, and Kenya Validated Trips datasets. Several components are part of Peskas, including peskas.timor.portal (R package), Peskas: Automated Analytics for Small-Scale Data-Deficient Fisheries (SoftwareX 2025), peskas.timor.pipeline (R package), Peskas Data Ingestion & Analytics Pipeline, Peskas Web Dashboard (Shiny), Peskas Automated Validation Engine, Peskas History & Global Scaling Timeline, Peskas Tracks App, and PESKAS \u2014 Digital Monitoring System for Small Scale Fisheries (slides). The Fishery Nutrient Profile (FNP) is also part of Peskas. Peskas shares data with Fishery Nutrient Profiles for Nutrition-Sensitive SSF Management in Timor-Leste (Nature Food 2026) and Beach Management Units (BMUs). Peskas is referenced by the WIOMSA Data Harmonization Presentation (Oct 2025), KoboToolbox, How Much Is Too Much Information? Digital Feedback on Fisher Behavior (Kenya), Dataverse Project, and FishBase. It is also conceptually related to COASTS (Small Scale Fisheries Analysis Platform), which is an inferred connection.",
